<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jean Chardin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1643">1643</a>–<a href="/years/1713">1713</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Sir John Chardin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jean Chardin was a French jeweller and traveller who made two long journeys to Safavid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> between 1665 and 1677, passing through Mingrelia, Imereti and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> in 1672–1673. His account, published from 1686, is one of the most detailed European descriptions of Safavid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and of the western Georgian principalities. A Huguenot, he settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, where Charles II knighted him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-chardin.jpg" alt="Jean Chardin" width="320" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to John Michael Wright · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean_Chardin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
